# Make a character, save WITHOUT quitting, keep playing, then save again on the
# way out. The point is the middle save: with INCURSION_SAVE_FAIL_AT set, that
# one throws part way through Registry::SaveGroup, and everything after it
# measures whether the game survived a failed save and stayed usable.
#
#   INCURSION_SAVE_FAIL_AT=5 tools/headless.sh tools/keys/save-fail.keys 1
#
# tools/check_save_fail.sh runs exactly this, at several failure points, and
# compares the character it ends with against the same session run with no
# failure staged. See bd inc-i0r.
#
# ESC b is the System Menu's [b] Save and Continue (src/Player.cpp:734-747).
# It is the only save the game offers that does not also end the session, so
# it is the only way a key script can carry on past one. READ THE WARNING IN
# tools/keys/explore-mage.keys BEFORE MOVING IT: ESC opens that menu rather
# than cancelling anything, [a] is Save and Quit, and a stray ESC therefore
# ends the run and still exits 0.
#
# The block below is placed where the screen is known -- straight after the
# arrival dump, with no other menu or prompt open -- for that reason.

@include chargen.keys

@dump:arrival

# A short walk before the save, so the character has moved and the save has
# something to be wrong about.
DOWN*4 RIGHT*4 UP*4 LEFT*4

ESC b   # System Menu -> Save and Continue. THIS is the save that is staged
        # to fail; the game must report the failure and keep playing.

@dump:after-save

# Play on. If the failed save left a handle where a data-block pointer belongs,
# the map walk below reads through it long before the session ends.
DOWN*4 RIGHT*4 UP*4 LEFT*4
DOWN*4 RIGHT*4 UP*4 LEFT*4
s*3     # search
.*5     # rest

@dump:walked

@quit   # Saves again on the way out. The staged failure fires once, so this
        # save must succeed and must produce a loadable file.
